# Release notes

Every release gets two texts from the same merged PRs: GitHub release notes, for developers and integrators (what changed in code and contract terms, with PR numbers), and the readme.txt changelog, for shop owners on WordPress.org and in the WP Admin "View details" window (what they will notice, in plain words). Nothing written for one audience is copied into the other. To adopt this file in another repo (WCPOS Pro, an extension), change only the product name, the repository URL and the script path.

## Workflow

Both texts are written in the release PR (`chore(release): X.Y.Z`), in this order:

1. List the PRs: `git log --first-parent --format='%h %s' vPREV..HEAD`, and read each PR body.
2. Classify each PR with the table below.
3. Write the GitHub notes in the release PR description under a `## GitHub release notes` heading.
4. Write the readme entry from the PRs marked "readme" and add it to the top of `== Changelog ==` in `readme.txt`.
5. Write or remove the Upgrade Notice, apply the retention rule, and run the pre-publish checklist.

Publishing: `release.yml` fills the draft GitHub release body from the readme entry (`.github/scripts/readme-changelog-section.sh`). If `release.yml` still calls `readme-changelog-section.sh` to fill the release body, whoever publishes the release (today Paul, or someone he names) does this before publishing: copy everything under `## GitHub release notes` in the merged release PR's description, up to the next `## ` heading or the end, without that heading, into `notes.md`; run `gh release edit vX.Y.Z --notes-file notes.md`; check that `gh release view vX.Y.Z` shows the `###` sections, no `= ` line and no readme wording.

Older line: a patch on an older line (for example 1.11.x) is written in that line's branch, in its own `readme.txt`, under the same rules, with retention and the Upgrade Notice counted for that line. It is never added to the newer line's readme. Its GitHub notes use the same template, with Full diff against the previous tag on that line.

## Classify each PR

| Each effect of a PR (classify every effect; the first matching row wins per effect) | readme | GitHub section |
|---|---|---|
| fixes a security hole, lost data, a lost sale or a failed payment | yes, first | Fixed |
| removes or loosens a conflict check, guard or protection that other plugins' data relied on | one line when a till can now overwrite store data a shop can see; otherwise no | Upgrade and compatibility |
| guards a case the same PR introduced | no | leave out, or a clause in the PR's Fixed bullet |
| adds an optional field or flag that changes nothing until an app release reads it | no | Developer and contract changes |
| changes what a cashier, manager or shop owner sees or does | yes | Added or Fixed |
| changes what the plugin stores about a sale or sends off the site | yes | Added or Fixed |
| makes a page measurably faster (the PR shows a measurement) | yes | Fixed |
| is a `perf` PR with no measurement | no | Fixed (say what work is skipped; no figure) |
| only adds or changes log lines | no, unless it completes a readme line from the same release | Fixed |
| changes the meaning, sign or range of an existing field, error, hook, filter, receipt data key or hash recipe so that some client, template or extension must act | one line when a shop can make the change itself (a receipt template edited in WP Admin); otherwise no | Upgrade and compatibility only |
| adds or changes a hook, filter, REST route or field, error code, option, capability, a public method Pro or a documented extension calls, the sync protocol or a receipt template data key (ADR 0039) | no (see Developers line) | Developer and contract changes |
| removes or renames any of those (a public method Pro or a documented extension calls included), or raises a minimum WordPress, WooCommerce or PHP version | one Developers line; a raised minimum is a normal line | Upgrade and compatibility |
| updates a bundled runtime library (anything in the shipped zip) | no | Maintenance |
| pins a new translation version | no | Maintenance: one line naming the last pin's version, citing every pin PR in the range |
| bumps the version, updates the `.pot`, bumps dev dependencies or CI actions, or changes only tests, specs or docs | no | leave out |
| anything else | no | leave out, and say why in the release PR |

"Added" takes `feat` PRs, "Fixed" takes `fix` and `perf` PRs.

## GitHub release notes

Template (headings are `###` so they sit under the PR's `## GitHub release notes`; leave out empty sections, keep this order):
```markdown
<One sentence: what the release adds and fixes, in technical terms. When Upgrade and compatibility is not empty, end with "Action may be needed: see Upgrade and compatibility.">

### Upgrade and compatibility
- <What someone must do, and who: raised minimums, removed or renamed contract, data migration, app update needed. 2.0.0 had: hooks renamed `wcpos_*`, old names still fire deprecated.>

### Added
- <New capability, named in code terms.> (#1234)

### Fixed
- <What failed, under what condition, and what the code does now.> (#1234)

### Developer and contract changes
- <Hook, filter, route, field, error code, option, capability or receipt data key, then "Additive" or the action needed.> (#1234)

### Maintenance
- <Bundled library version or translation pin.> (#1234)

Full diff: https://github.com/wcpos/woocommerce-pos/compare/vPREV...vX.Y.Z
```

Do:
- One change per bullet, present tense, ending with its PR number(s) in parentheses. A number that is not a PR in this release is written "issue #N", never bare in parentheses.
- Put names a developer types in backticks: hooks, filters, routes, fields, error codes, options, capabilities, meta keys, functions.
- End every contract bullet with "Additive" (older apps and code ignore it) or the action needed (a recipe change: see the next rule), and for a removal name the version it goes in (deprecate in X.Y, remove in X.Y+2 at the earliest). A new error code ends "New error code on <where>.", with client guidance only if the PR gives it.
- Start a change to how a revision, hash or digest is computed with "The <name> recipe changes", and say what happens to values clients already hold; if the PR does not say, write "<Name> hashes change for orders carrying such meta".
- Put security fixes first in Fixed, starting "Security:", then data-loss and payment fixes.
- Quote a figure only as a before/after pair, before measured on the previous release and after on the merged head; otherwise quote nothing.

Don't:
- Use bold, emoji, marketing words ("major", "much faster", "huge") or "various fixes"; paste the readme lines (write the technical cause and effect); cite internal tracker ids (Sentry issue ids, `POS-…`).
- List release commits, `.pot` updates, dev-dependency or CI-action bumps, or test-only PRs.

### Worked example: 2.0.3

```markdown
Stops other plugins' order meta from failing POS checkouts, and cuts the cost of the POS Only exclusion on storefront product queries.

### Fixed
- Order pushes no longer fail as "Checkout failed" (`provenance_save_failed`) when another plugin writes hidden order or line meta after each push or on read: the order revision hashes core fields, line items, visible meta and only the allow-listed hidden keys. (#2664)
- A push no longer overwrites another plugin's hidden meta that already exists on the server order or line, so a stale till cannot revert a tracking number. New keys are still written; `_woocommerce_pos_uuid` is exempt. (#2664)
- Storefront product and variation queries carry the POS Only list once, built once per request, instead of twice and rebuilt per query. (#2662)
- `post__not_in` and the `post__in` trim are kept on storefront product queries, so ElasticPress, Relevanssi, SearchWP and Algolia still exclude POS Only products. (#2662)

### Developer and contract changes
- New filter `wcpos_sync_order_revision_excluded_meta_keys` leaves meta keys out of the order revision (order, line, shipping, fee and coupon line meta). Default empty, so hashes are unchanged. Additive. (#2663)
- New filter `wcpos_sync_order_revision_meta_keys` lists the hidden meta keys kept in the revision. Additive. (#2664)
- The order revision recipe changes. Push and money-lane compares also accept a base equal to the previous recipe, so acks issued before 2.0.3 stay valid; the fallback is removed in 2.2 at the earliest. No client action needed. (#2664)

Full diff: https://github.com/wcpos/woocommerce-pos/compare/v2.0.2...v2.0.3
```

### Worked example: 2.0.4

```markdown
Receipts print per-item taxes as charged, the Plugins-screen upgrade notice renders as HTML, and order-push conflicts name the fields that differ.

### Fixed
- Receipts print each item's tax with the label and rate stored on the order's tax items, so a reprint after a rate was renamed (WooCommerce Tax does this) matches the amount charged. (#2675)
- The WordPress.org upgrade notice in the Plugins-screen update row is printed through `wp_kses_post()` instead of `esc_html()`, in a `<div>`, so the "Important" box shows formatted text instead of tags. (#2641)
- Integrity digest failures are logged under one fixed message with the database error in the log context, so error reporting groups them as one issue instead of one per object. (#2660)

### Developer and contract changes
- An order push refused with 409 now includes `differs`: the sorted paths where the pushed order and the server copy disagree in the revision's canonical form (names only, never values). The hash, `current` and `currentRevision` are unchanged. Additive. (#2668)

### Maintenance
- Translation version pinned to 2026.11.19. (#2669, #2676, #2680)

Full diff: https://github.com/wcpos/woocommerce-pos/compare/v2.0.3...v2.0.4
```

### Worked example: 2.0.5

```markdown
Lets a manager reassign a sale to another cashier, and fixes guest payments on WooCommerce 11.3.1, several push and sign-in failures, and sync tables that go missing. Action may be needed: see Upgrade and compatibility.

### Upgrade and compatibility
- Custom receipt templates: a returned line's `qty` is now negative and `total_qty` is the net count; templates that assume a positive `qty` must handle the sign. (#2684)
- Extensions that store visible order-level meta (custom order fields): a push now writes the till's value for any visible order-level key not in `wcpos_sync_order_revision_meta_keys` instead of refusing on conflict; existing hidden keys off the list are still never overwritten. To keep a visible key conflict-checked, add it to that filter, which at order level now lists every hashed key, visible or hidden (in 2.0.3 it listed hidden keys only). (#2713)

### Added
- Users with `reassign_woocommerce_pos_sales` can set `_pos_user` on a pushed order to another existing POS user; other `_pos_user` values are still ignored. (#2719)
- A cashier or store change on an order with a sale record, from a push or the WP Admin cashier select, appends a reattribution fiscal record (who, when, from, to, `corrects_record_id`). If the record cannot be written, the change is not applied and an order note says so. (#2719)
- Opt-in fatal-error events sent to WCPOS's error reporting carry the request kind, its REST route and a path with token-like segments masked. (#2700)

### Fixed
- Guest-order payments on the pay page work again on WooCommerce 11.3.1, whose new order-pay email check dropped them once the order was more than 10 minutes old. After WCPOS authenticates the request (order key and cashier JWT), `woocommerce_order_email_verification_required` returns false for that order in the `order-pay` context only. (#2698)
- POS customer creates forwarded to `wc/v3/customers` get a generated username and password, so they no longer fail with "Missing parameter(s): username" when the store does not generate them. (#2704)
- A re-pushed `pos-discount` line that drops its intent keeps the stored quick discount instead of failing with "pos-discount does not exist"; an edited or deleted quick discount inherits nothing. (#2703)
- Pushes no longer 409 on every attempt when another plugin serves order meta whose value changes on read (one served an unsaved row holding the clock, so no order could check out). (#2713)
- A sync table (journal, stored digest, mutations) dropped after install is re-created under the DB upgrade lock, instead of every gated sync request answering 503 `wcpos_sync_unavailable`. When a table cannot be created, its name and the database error are logged. (#2712, #2711)
- A surviving journal keeps its epoch and watermarks during that repair, so clients do not resync; only a missing journal starts a new epoch. (#2712)
- Session-row writes are compare-and-swap, so overlapping sign-ins and sign-outs of one user no longer lose a sign-in or bring back a signed-out session. (#2656)
- Receipt data keeps a returned line's signed quantity and unit price instead of 0 at 0.00, and the fiscal sale snapshot stores the signed quantity; returned lines carry no savings and saved totals are clamped at 0. (#2684)
- POS Only hooks return early on non-product queries and skip a redundant re-check. (#2699)

### Developer and contract changes
- The order revision recipe changes: order-level meta is hashed only for the keys that filter lists (see Upgrade and compatibility), and a meta row served with a numeric non-positive id is outside the hash on orders and lines (its echo in a push is dropped, not stored). Line meta hashing is otherwise unchanged. Revision hashes change for orders carrying such meta. (#2713)
- A sign-in whose session row cannot be stored answers 503 `woocommerce_pos_auth_session_not_stored` instead of a token pair the next request refuses as revoked. New error code on sign-in. (#2656)
- The payment-methods envelope adds `default_method`, the admin's default gateway, so the app can preselect it. Contract stays 1.3; older apps ignore it. Additive. (#2681)
- The status route advertises `order_cashier_reassignment`. Additive. (#2719)
- New filter `wcpos_error_reporting_request_context` turns off the request context on fatal-error events. Additive. (#2700)

### Maintenance
- Bundled Sentry PHP SDK 4.37.1, with `guzzlehttp/psr7` held at 3.2.0 until the scoped build handles its `trigger_deprecation()` call (tracked in issue #2678). (#2697, #2671)
- Translation version pinned to 2026.11.21. (#2682, #2683)

Full diff: https://github.com/wcpos/woocommerce-pos/compare/v2.0.4...v2.0.5
```

## readme.txt changelog

Template:
```text
= X.Y.Z - YYYY/MM/DD =

* <What the shop owner notices, in one sentence.>
* New: <a new thing they can do.>
* Developers: <only a removed or renamed hook, filter or route.>
```

An X.Y.0 entry may open with up to three plain paragraphs before the list (what changed, what to do first, a link), each at most two sentences and 250 characters; its list holds new features without the "New:" label, then removals or new requirements, then the Developers line. Fixes for beta testers follow a plain line such as "For testers coming from the 2.0 beta 3:".

Order of lines: (1) security fixes, starting "Security:"; (2) fixes where a sale, payment, sign-in or stored record (an order, a customer, an edit) failed, was lost or was stored wrong; (3) "New:" lines; (4) other fixes and changes, including a loosened-guard line from the table (a till can now overwrite store data); (5) speed and appearance; (6) the Developers line. Within a group, what most shops notice comes first. A line that asks the shop to act starts "If you …" and goes directly after the line for the same PR, or after group (2) when there is none.

Do:
- Start with what the reader sees or does: "Receipts show…", "Paying for a guest order… works again", "Sales no longer fail with…". Write one sentence per line, present tense, at most 150 characters, ending with a full stop. A fix reads as a fix ("no longer", "again"); only new abilities get "New:".
- Quote on-screen error text exactly, in double quotes, so merchants can search for it. Name screens as the cashier or WP Admin shows them (the pay page, POS > Checkout), never by a generic word, and the WooCommerce version a fix depends on. Use one name for a screen across the whole changelog, the name earlier entries already use; when a new entry finds two, rename the older lines to match.
- Mark Pro-only features "(Pro)". A line about data sent off the site names who receives it and says if it is opt-in.

Don't:
- Use bold or italics anywhere in the changelog. Use no emoji, no all-caps words (acronyms such as CSV are fine) and no backticks except on the Developers line.
- Write PR or issue numbers, tracker ids, HTTP codes, hook, class or field names, or internal words (revision, envelope, allow-list, journal, 409, gateway, pane, CDN, fiscal record, sync table, token, payload, event, fatal error, request, path), or mention version bumps, `.pot` updates, translation pins, dependency updates, CI or tests.
- Start any line inside an entry with `=`: a `= ` line ends extraction early, and an `==` line does not stop it, so it leaks into the body. Use only `= X.Y.Z - YYYY/MM/DD =` headings, `* ` bullets, plain paragraphs and `[text](url)` links.
- Inflate: no "major" (except for an X.0.0 release), "much faster" or "various improvements", and no number or scope ("every", "always") the PR does not state.

Developers line: add one closing "Developers:" line only when a site with custom code must act (a hook, filter, REST route or public method removed or renamed). Everything else developer-facing is GitHub only.

Retention: the changelog keeps the current minor line only: its X.Y.0 entry and its newest patch entries, at most six entries in all, newest first (drop the oldest patch first). When X.Y+1.0 or X+1.0.0 ships, the previous line's entries leave the readme. The section opens with this line, above the first entry, so the extraction script never picks it up: `Older releases are listed on [GitHub](https://github.com/wcpos/woocommerce-pos/releases).`

Upgrade Notice: the Plugins screen shows only the notice WordPress.org sends for the version on offer, so `== Upgrade Notice ==` holds one notice, `= X.Y.Z =` for the new release, and the previous notice is deleted (its text stays in its changelog entry). Write one when merchants must act before updating or should wait: sync first, retrain cashiers, a raised minimum version, the app must update too. While the current line's X.Y.0 entry asks merchants to act first, every patch on that line repeats a short notice for sites coming from the previous line, after the patch's own action if it has one. When both notices do not fit in 300 characters, drop the link first, then cut the carried-over notice to its sync sentence. One plain-text paragraph of at most 300 characters including any URL; no Markdown, no HTML; every claim comes from a changelog entry or the readme header. With no notice due, the section heading stays and nothing follows it.

### Worked example: 2.0.5 Upgrade Notice

```text
== Upgrade Notice ==

= 2.0.5 =
Coming from 1.11? WCPOS 2.0 is a major update with a new interface: update when you have time, not during a busy shift. Sync every till first: unsent 1.11 sales do not carry over. The app must be on 2.0 too, and 2.0 needs WooCommerce 9.0 or later. https://docs.wcpos.com/getting-started/whats-new
```

### Worked example: 2.0.3

```text
= 2.0.3 - 2026/11/13 =

* Sales no longer fail with "Checkout failed" when another plugin keeps updating its own hidden data on an order.
* A till no longer overwrites another plugin's hidden order data, such as a tracking number, with an older copy.
* Product lists in the online shop load faster on stores with tens of thousands of POS Only products.
```

### Worked example: 2.0.4

```text
= 2.0.4 - 2026/11/15 =

* Receipts show each item's tax with the name and rate the order was charged, even after the tax rate is renamed (WooCommerce Tax does this).
* The orange "Important" upgrade notice on the Plugins screen shows formatted text instead of raw HTML tags.
```

### Worked example: 2.0.5

```text
= 2.0.5 - 2026/11/20 =

* Paying for a guest order on the pay page works again on WooCommerce 11.3.1, which dropped the payment once the order was over 10 minutes old.
* Adding a customer at the till no longer fails with "Missing parameter(s): username" when WooCommerce does not generate usernames or passwords itself.
* Checking out an order with a quick discount no longer fails with "pos-discount does not exist" when the till sends the order again.
* Sales no longer fail to check out when another plugin shows order data that keeps changing, such as the current time.
* If a cleanup plugin or a partial restore deletes a table WCPOS uses for syncing, WCPOS re-creates it so tills sync again, or logs why it cannot.
* Sign-ins and sign-outs of the same user that happen at the same moment no longer undo each other.
* If the store cannot save a sign-in, it now fails straight away instead of seeming to work and failing on the next step.
* A returned item prints on the receipt and is saved with the sale at its negative quantity and its price, not quantity 0 at 0.00.
* If you edited a receipt template, test it with a return: a returned item's quantity is now negative, which the template must handle.
* New: a manager can move a sale to another cashier, and a completed sale keeps a record of the change.
* When a cashier edits a custom order field at the till, the till's value is saved, even if the field was changed in the store since the last sync.
* If you chose to send error reports to WCPOS, crash reports now say what the store was doing, hiding anything in the web address that looks like a key.
```

## Pre-publish checklist

- [ ] Every PR in `vPREV..HEAD` is in the GitHub notes, or is left out with its row of the table named in the release PR. Every readme line traces to a PR in the GitHub notes, and every number in either text appears in a PR body, measured on the merged head or on the previous release.
- [ ] Security, data-loss and payment fixes are in both texts, first in their group.
- [ ] Every bullet under Developer and contract changes says "Additive", names the action, says "New error code on …", or (for a recipe change) says what happens to held values; `grep -n '\*\*'` over the changelog section finds nothing, nor does a grep for italic emphasis (`*word*`, `_word_`): `grep -nE '(^|[ (])[*_][^ *_][^*_]*[*_]([ .,;:)]|$)'`, and no readme bullet is over 150 characters after its `* `.
- [ ] `readme-changelog-section.sh X.Y.Z` prints exactly the new entry. Run it for the oldest kept entry too: after that entry's last line only `== Upgrade Notice ==` may follow (the script does not stop at `==`). A `release.yml` re-run for that oldest version would publish that `== Upgrade Notice ==` line, so remove it from that release body by hand.
- [ ] The translation pin named in Maintenance is the last pin PR in the range. Retention applied, the opening GitHub line present, and the Upgrade Notice is one `= X.Y.Z =` for this release (or none), at most 300 characters of plain text.
- [ ] If `release.yml` still fills the body from the readme, the publisher has run `gh release edit vX.Y.Z --notes-file notes.md` and checked the result.

References: WordPress.org example readme (markup, Upgrade Notice limit): https://wordpress.org/plugins/readme.txt; How your readme.txt works (10 KB warning): https://developer.wordpress.org/plugins/wordpress-org/how-your-readme-txt-works/; Keep a Changelog 1.1.0: https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/. Reference changelogs, none bolding items: Query Monitor, WooCommerce, Akismet, Jetpack and Yoast SEO, each at https://wordpress.org/plugins/<slug>/#developers
